# {{ organization }}

Data repository following the [Statera](https://github.com/Jaimenms/statera) standard: each statistic is a
**data product** with a contract, information classification, tested rules, an executable process and a
record of each published edition.

```
content/        what the team edits: products, reference tables, queries, recipes, governance
publications/   editions register (written by the process, not by hand)
docs/           studies, technical notes and decisions (free text)
pyproject.toml  the pinned Statera version (uv.lock fixes it exactly)
```

## Only want to query the data?

You do not need the production tools (dbt, legacy-code readers): only [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/).

```bash
uv sync                                  # installs only what reading needs
uv run statera doctor                    # says where the data comes from (or whom to ask)
uv run statera query list                # cataloged queries
uv run statera query run <id> --param uf=SP --output result/
```

In Python (notebook or script, with `uv run`):

```python
from statera.consume import catalog

cat = catalog.open()
cat.search(layer="gold")                     # what exists, with classification
cat.table("gold.<table>").to_pandas()        # current data
cat.edition("<product>", "2026-06")          # exactly what was published, hash checked
```

The data comes from the shared engine in `content/statera.toml` (`[consumption] engine`); if it needs
credentials, the data team gives you a URL to put in the `STATERA_ENGINE` environment variable (never in
git). The catalog of tables, queries and metrics is in [content/docs/](content/docs/README.md).

## Getting started (data team)

Requires [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), which installs Python, Statera and the other dependencies. It
works the same on Windows, Linux and Mac.

```bash
# once per computer
uv tool install "statera[produce] @ git+{{ statera_git }}"   # the `statera` command; later: uv tool upgrade statera

# in this repository, from any folder
statera doctor    # checks the computer and the repository, and says how to fix each problem
statera check     # what CI runs: validation, lint and fast tests
```

`statera` always runs the version this repository pins: the global command finds the repository root and
hands off to it (`uv run statera …`), updating the environment when needed. `uv run statera …` works too,
with nothing installed globally.

In VS Code, open this folder and install the recommended extensions: YAML files are then validated as you
type, and the commands below are available as tasks (Terminal › Run Task…; Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+B runs
`statera check`).

## Day to day

| I want to… | Command |
|---|---|
| check my environment | `statera doctor` |
| create a new statistic | `statera new-product my-statistic` |
| see what a product is missing | `statera conformance` |
| regenerate the generated files after editing | `statera sync` |
| check everything before the pull request | `statera check` (`--all` also runs the slow tests) |
| produce an edition | `statera run --product my-statistic --period 2026-06 --with-dependencies` |
| complete a human task (approval) and continue | `statera resume --product my-statistic --period 2026-06` |
| documentation and browsable catalog | `statera docs` · `statera docs --interactive` |

All commands: `statera --help`.

## Where everything lives

```
content/
  statera.toml          repository configuration (engine, column conventions, register, required version)
  products/<product>/   one folder per statistic, organized by GSBPM phase:
    1-specify/          ODCS contract, ODPS product, classification, owners, calendar
    2-design/           process (YAML mirror of the BPMN)
    4-collect/          extraction from the sources
    5-process/          dbt models (SQL or Python), rules with test cases, metrics
    6-analyse/          controls and statistical disclosure control
    7-disseminate/      published series
    8-evaluate/         quality indicators
  references/           shared reference tables (classifications, territories…)
  queries/ recipes/     recurring queries and examples of data use
  governance/           roles and shared owners (they generate .github/CODEOWNERS)
```

Files marked as generated (`GENERATED FILE` notice at the top) are not edited by hand: `statera sync`
rebuilds them.

## Who installs what

`pyproject.toml` lists as dependencies only what reading the data needs. The production tools are
dependency groups: `produce` (dbt, Copier), `dev` (pytest, ruff) and, when the products have legacy code,
`legacy-r` and `legacy-spss` (uncomment them in `pyproject.toml`; `statera doctor` tells you when one is
missing). The `statera` commands that produce install the groups by themselves; `uv sync --all-groups`
installs them all at once.

## Updating Statera

The version is in `pyproject.toml` (`tag = "v{{ statera_version }}"`) and the accepted range in
`content/statera.toml` (`required_version`). To update, change both, run `statera check` (uv updates the
environment and uv.lock) and open a pull request.
